Qt
--

Qt 6.11.2, The Qt Company's official macOS binaries, redistributed under the
GNU Lesser General Public License version 3.0 only. Qt is available under
several licenses; LGPL-3.0-only is the one Musix relies on. LGPL-3.0
incorporates the GNU General Public License version 3, so both texts are
included:

  Qt 6.11.2                 LGPL-3.0-only      Qt-LGPL-3.0.txt
                                               Qt-LGPL-3.0-incorporates-GPL-3.0.txt

The bundle ships Qt's frameworks and the plugins they load, and nothing else
alongside them: every framework in Musix.app/Contents/Frameworks is Qt's, and
no other shared library is bundled. The build checks this and fails otherwise.

Qt compiles third-party code into its own binaries. Qt records each piece in a
qt_attribution.json beside it; the notices and full license texts for every
component that can be in the shipped frameworks and plugins are collected from
those files, out of the pinned Qt 6.11.2 module sources, into

                                               Qt-third-party.txt

That includes, among others, HarfBuzz, libpng, libjpeg-turbo, libwebp, PCRE2,
FreeType, MD4C, TinyCBOR, double-conversion, the JavaScriptCore macro
assembler, Yoga, Pixman, the Unicode Character Database and CLDR, and the
Public Suffix List. scripts/qt-licenses.py writes it and states, for each
component it leaves out, why that code cannot be in the bundle -- other
platforms, build tooling, tests, and the parts of Qt the bundle does not
carry -- and the build checks each of those premises against the bundle.

Where one of those components is offered under more than one license, the
option Musix takes is:

  FreeType, and its anti-aliasing rasterizer in QtGui
             FTL OR GPL-2.0-only; taken under the FreeType License (FTL).
  libdbus-1 headers
             AFL-2.1 OR GPL-2.0-or-later; taken under AFL-2.1.
  BLAKE2     CC0-1.0 OR Apache-2.0; taken under CC0-1.0.
  DR Libs    Unlicense OR MIT-0; taken under MIT-0.
  Vulkan API Registry
             Apache-2.0 OR MIT; taken under MIT.

No GPL-licensed component is redistributed.

